# shir_nodes/substr_extract.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# Extract a substring (lowered from `expr substr`, `${var:N:M}`).
#
# Backends: Perl substr(), JS slice(), C strncpy, Rust &s[n..n+m]
# Fallback: sh2.subStr(text, offset, length)
#
# ShIR_TextOps_Strings proves `substr_extract_sound`: the byte-at-a-time scan a
# C arm performs equals `take length (drop offset text)`, which is what the JS,
# Perl and Rust arms compute; the result is never longer than `length`, a zero
# length is empty, and an offset past the end is the empty string.
# NEGATIVE offsets and lengths (`${v: -3}`, `${v:0:-2}`) are modelled in
# docs/fm/TextOps/ShIR_TextOps_Param.thy, where `start_of` / `stop_of` clamp
# exactly as bash does: `slice_nonneg` recovers the non-negative law above, so
# the negative forms EXTEND the proven model instead of sitting beside it;
# `slice_from_last_n` is `${v: -n}` (the last n bytes, clamped to the whole
# string), `slice_negative_length` is `${v:0:-n}` (drop the last n),
# `slice_from_neg_clamps` / `slice_neg_len_clamps` are the past-the-end clamps,
# and `negative_offset_is_not_zero` / `negative_length_is_not_ignored` /
# `negative_length_is_not_a_prefix` REFUTE the three readings that treat a
# negative argument as 0, as the whole string, or as a prefix length. What
# remains outside: `${v:off:len}` with an offset expressed relative to the
# END and a length counted FORWARD (`${v: -3:2}`) is the two-index form with
# both arms negative, which `slice` covers by construction but only at the
# shapes above; quoting and locale are outside the fragment altogether.

node SubStrExtract
tag "SubStrExtract"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field text: expr
field offset: expr
field length: optional_expr
spec: TextOps#substr_extract_sound
spec: TextOps#slice_nonneg
spec: TextOps#slice_from_last_n
spec: TextOps#slice_negative_length
spec: TextOps#negative_offset_is_not_zero
spec: TextOps#negative_length_is_not_ignored
spec: TextOps#negative_length_is_not_a_prefix
